Morocco has an identity problem. The erosion of its Berber/Amazigh inheritance, indifference toward its ancient and Roman past, the dominance of Arab media over local cultural production, proliferation of Islamists, and poor knowledge of the Kingdom’s history have convinced too many Moroccans that their civilization lies somewhere to the east when it never did.
Morocco spent centuries resisting conquest and was the only country in North Africa never incorporated into the Ottoman Empire. Moroccan rulers dealt pragmatically with Portugal and Spain when Ottoman expansion threatened their independence, while the other dynasties’ relationship with England for example included trade which directly served Morocco’s military power. This is also why so much of anti-Western talking points are incompatible with Morocco’s own history. If Moroccans forget what their ancestors spent centuries defending, all that resistance will have been in vain.

